<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francis James Child</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a>–<a href="/years/1896">1896</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francis James Child was an American scholar and folklorist at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>, known for The English and Scottish Popular Ballads, the collection now called the Child Ballads. He became Boylston Professor of Rhetoric and Oratory in 1851, and in 1876 Harvard made him its first professor of English to keep him from Johns Hopkins. Using the methods of comparative philology and corresponding with the Danish scholar Svend Grundtvig, he gathered some 305 ballads with their variants, published in parts from 1882 to 1898, the last after his death in 1896. His studies also shaped how Chaucer's grammar and pronunciation are understood, and in 1888 he founded the American Folklore Society.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francis-james-child.jpg" alt="Francis James Child" width="330" height="467" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francis_J._Child.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Francis James Child was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 1 February 1825, the son of a sailmaker. The family was poor, but he was educated in Boston's free public schools, and Epes Sargent Dixwell, principal of the Boston Latin School, saw that he received a scholarship to Harvard. He graduated in 1846 at the top of his class, was chosen class orator and became a tutor in mathematics, then in history, political economy and English literature. In 1848 he published an annotated edition of Four Old Plays, the first of its kind in America, and a loan from Jonathan I. Bowditch let him study English drama and Germanic philology at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>, where he heard lectures by the linguists Grimm.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As Boylston Professor he supervised a 130-volume series of the British poets from 1853, editing Edmund Spenser and the English and Scottish Ballads (1857–1858) himself. Finding that no sound early text of Chaucer existed, he wrote Observations on the Language of Chaucer (1863), and his research largely shaped how Chaucer's grammar, pronunciation and scansion are now understood. In the 1860s he campaigned for the Early English Text Society of Frederick James Furnivall to publish Percy's Folio, which appeared in 1868, and with Furnivall founded the Ballad Society.</p><p>When Daniel Coit Gilman offered him a research chair at Johns Hopkins in 1876, Harvard's president Charles William Eliot freed him from correcting compositions so that he could research. Child believed the ballads came from a time when society was less divided by class, and by the people he meant all classes. Besides Grundtvig he consulted scholars such as the Sicilian folklorist Giuseppe Pitrè, and his references draw on thirty languages.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a> on 11 September 1896, his collection complete except for its planned introduction and bibliography. His student and successor George Lyman Kittredge wrote a biographical introduction and saw himself as keeper of Child's legacy. Child left Harvard one of the largest folklore collections in existence. An edition of 2002 printed his rediscovered essay Ballad Poetry of 1874, which answers the charge that his selection was arbitrary. Steve Roud's Roud Folk Song Index later absorbed and extended his sources. He is buried at Stockbridge, Massachusetts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francis James Child” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 19th century <b>Francis James Child</b> combined these and versions from the Roxburghe Ballads into the standard texts used by later scholars.</blockquote><a href="/songs/barbara-allen">Barbara Allen</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words have much in common with the Scottish ballad &quot;The Elfin Knight&quot;, which <b>Francis James Child</b> collected as his second ballad and which has been traced back to 1670; in it an elf threatens to carry off a young woman unless she performs an impossible task, and…</blockquote><a href="/songs/scarborough-fair">Scarborough Fair</a></li><li><blockquote>Newer versions, with different music, were taken down up to the 19th century, and <b>Francis James Child</b> printed several of them as number 26 of his Child Ballads.</blockquote><a href="/songs/the-three-ravens">The Three Ravens</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boston">Boston</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.36,-71.06&amp;year=1871" aria-label="Boston on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2827 657 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francis James Child"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3277 927h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3277 927h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.36,-71.06&amp;year=1871">What was here in 1871? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
